            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"God's judgment and temporary covenantal separation from unfaithful Israel due to spiritual adultery (idolatry).\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Jeremiah 3:8\",\"Isaiah 50:1\",\"Hosea 2:2-4\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"A literal ethical and legal provision concerning human marriage, marital unfaithfulness, and the hardness of the human heart.\",\"basis\":[\"Deuteronomy 24:1-4\",\"Matthew 19:7-9\",\"Mark 10:4-12\"],\"prominence\":\"dominant\"},{\"meaning\":\"The termination of the Old Covenant system (the Law) to establish the New Covenant (grace and the Church).\",\"basis\":[\"Romans 7:1-4\",\"Galatians 4:21-31\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"Total apostasy or the final spiritual severing of an individual or institution from God's grace.\",\"basis\":[\"Revelation 2:22-23\"],\"prominence\":\"rare\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"In prophetic literature, a bill of divorcement is the established symbol for God formally rejecting a rebellious nation (usually the northern kingdom of Israel) because of idolatrous spiritual adultery, though He often promises future restoration. In the New Testament, discussions of the bill of divorcement are almost entirely literal and ethical rather than symbolic.\"}",
